course: everyday
lesson: 429
title: The forged signature and how it always ends
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Jack in Liverpool got a note home about a fight in the yard. Scared of what Mum would say, he signed it himself with her name and handed it in. His teacher took one look and rang his mum. Now there were two problems: the fight AND the fake signature. Jack was grounded for two weeks, lost his phone, and for the rest of the year the school checked every note he brought.
explain: Signing someone else's name to fool people is called forgery, and it is a serious kind of lie, because the paper says a grown-up agreed when they didn't. It always ends the same way: paper goes home, schools phone parents, parents talk to teachers. Arjun in Mumbai wrote his own permission slip for a trip his parents had said no to; on the morning of the trip the teacher phoned home about pick-up time, and he was taken off the bus in front of everyone. Ryan's mate in Brisbane bragged «done it heaps», until the school found one forged note and then found them all, and he was suspended (kept off school) for a day.
explain: Other kids hide or change papers instead. Lily «lost» two signed tests on the bus; the teacher emailed both parents, who found them screwed up in her bag; she went through them with Dad, which helped. Sophie changed «needs improvement» on her report to «needs no improvement»; her dad, a teacher himself, laughed out loud, then stopped laughing. Emma's mum trusted her to tick her own reading log, and Emma ticked days she hadn't read; her reading level stayed still, she owned up, and breaking Mum's trust hurt more than any punishment.
explain: Often the paper hides a smaller, real problem. Ollie in Belfast forged a sick note to skip swimming because he was scared of the deep end. That night his PE teacher met his mum at the shops. Mum was cross about the note, but sad he hadn't told her he was scared — and the teacher let him start in the shallow end.
explain: Ms Rivera, a teacher in New York, tells her class about the note she forged when she was ten: «Paper always goes home in the end» — sooner or later a faked paper reaches the real parents. After hearing the story, one girl admitted she had signed a note herself, and Ms Rivera helped her tell her mum.
explain: Kayla in Atlanta found a form about her missing homework that needed Mom's signature by Friday. She thought about signing it herself for a whole evening. Then she put it on the kitchen table and went to bed. Mom signed it, sighed, and they made a homework plan. «Y'all could've had a way worse week,» said her big brother, who had once tried the other way.
fact: Forgery means signing someone else's name, or faking a paper, to make people believe it is real.
fact: A forged signature on a school note is a serious lie, because it pretends a parent agreed.
fact: Schools often check notes by phoning or emailing parents, so a faked note is usually found.
fact: Once one forged note is found, a school can go back and check all the others.
fact: Forging a note turns one problem into two: the thing in the note and the forgery.
fact: Hiding a signed paper or test does not stop the school, which can email parents directly.
fact: Changing the words on a report is faking an official paper and is taken very seriously.
fact: When someone trusts you to tick or sign for yourself, using it to lie breaks that trust.
fact: A bad mark is information that shows what to practise; hiding it blocks help.
fact: A forged note often hides a smaller real problem, like fear, that has an easy fix.
fact: If you have already faked a paper, telling a trusted grown-up now is better than waiting for it to be found.
fact: The hardest part of handing in a bad note is putting it on the table; after that it is just a talk.
exercise: What was the note Jack signed himself about? | A fight in the yard | Homework he hadn't handed in for a week | A trip to the museum | Being late for the swimming lesson
exercise: How was Jack's fake signature found out? | His teacher looked and rang his mum | His mum found the note in his school bag | A classmate saw him and told the teacher | The school posted a letter home a month later
exercise: After the forgery, how many problems did Jack have? | Two: the fight and the forgery | One: only the fight in the yard | None, the teacher sorted it all | Three: the fight, the bus and the phone
exercise: What did Lily do with her signed tests? | Hid them and said she lost them on the bus | Signed them herself with her mum's name on top | Gave them to her friend to keep at her house | Threw them in the big bin behind the school kitchen
exercise: What actually happened after Lily's parents saw the tests? | She went through them with Dad, which helped | She was moved down to an easier class at once | She was allowed to skip the next few tests | Nothing at all, they didn't mind the marks
exercise: How was Arjun's home-made permission slip found out? | The teacher phoned home about pick-up time | The bus driver checked the writing against an old note | His friends told the teacher about it | His mum found the pen he had used
exercise: What did Sophie change on her report card? | «Needs improvement» to «needs no improvement» | Her mark in maths from C to A | Her name to her sister's | The date of the report
exercise: What happened to Ryan's mate, who said «done it heaps»? | The school found all his notes and suspended him for a day | Nobody ever noticed a single thing, all year long, so he kept going | He became the note writer for half the class | His teacher praised his grown-up handwriting
exercise: What did Emma do with the reading log Mum trusted her to tick? | Ticked days she hadn't read | Ticked only the days she really read | Asked her teacher to tick it | Threw the log away
exercise: What gave Emma away? | Her reading level wasn't moving | Mum walked in and saw her ticking | The ticks were all in red pen | Her teacher counted the books
exercise: What did Ollie's forged sick note really hide? | That he was scared of the deep end | That he had lost his swimming kit again | That he was really ill with a bad cold | That he didn't like the swimming teacher
exercise: Where was Ollie's forged note found out? | His PE teacher met his mum at the shops | The school nurse rang his home to check | At the library, where he was seen | His friend told the whole class at lunch
exercise: What happened after a girl heard Ms Rivera's story? | She admitted signing a note and got help telling her mum | She signed another note, just for fun this time | She told the librarian about Ms Rivera | She laughed, said it was made up and forgot about it
exercise: What did Kayla do with the form in the end? | Put it on the kitchen table for Mom | Signed it herself late on Friday night | Hid it at the bottom of her bag | Threw it in the bin
exercise: What is forgery? | Signing someone else's name to make a paper look real | Writing a note in very neat joined-up handwriting | Forgetting to bring a signed note back to school | Losing an important paper on the bus
exercise: Why do forged notes nearly always get found? | Schools contact parents, and paper goes home | Teachers can always tell just by looking at your face | Every note at school is checked for fingerprints | Forged notes fade after a few days, so they get noticed
test: Mate in Brisbane: «easy, I sign my own notes, done it heaps». What does «done it heaps» really mean for him? | A bigger pile waiting to be found | Proof that it's safe and nobody checks | That he's really good at school | That teachers don't care
test: Ms Rivera: «Paper always goes home in the end.» What does she mean? | Faked papers reach the real parents sooner or later | Paper is always sent home by post | Homework should never be left at school overnight | Notes always get lost on the way home
test: Kayla's brother: «Y'all could've had a way worse week.» Why is he right? | A forged form would have meant far more trouble | The homework plan was a punishment worse than any | Mom would never have checked | He wanted to sign the form himself
test: What would have happened if Ollie had told Mum he was scared of the deep end? | He could have started in the shallow end with no lie | He'd have been made to dive in on the first day | He'd have been banned from swimming for good | Nothing at all would have changed
test: «my report says needs improvement, I'll just change it». Why is that a terrible idea? | Changing an official paper is a serious lie and will be checked | Reports are written in a special ink that can never be changed | Parents never read reports anyway | It's fine if you change only one small word
test: Lily: «if they never see the test, they never know». Which is TRUE? | Teachers can email parents directly | Tests can't be checked if lost | Bad marks vanish when hidden | Parents never ask about tests
test: Emma's mum: «just tick it yourself, I trust you». What's the honest way to use that trust? | Tick only the days she really read | Tick every day to save time | Tick only the days when Mum is away | Never tick anything at all
test: Arjun's parents said no to the trip. What's a better move than faking a slip? | Ask why, and try asking again honestly | Forge the slip again but with neater writing | Go on the trip without telling anyone | Ask a friend's parent to sign it
test: Jack, shaking: «If I show Mum the note about the fight, she'll go mad.» What's the wise choice? | Give her the note — one problem is better than two | Sign it for her, to save her the stress of it all | Lose it on the way home | Tell the teacher that Mum is away
test: You already faked a note last week. What is the best thing to do now? | Tell a trusted grown-up before the school finds it | Wait and hope nobody checks | Fake another to cover the first | Ask a friend to keep watch
test: In a film, a kid forges a parent's signature and is never caught. What is closer to real life? | Schools phone parents and the forgery is usually found | Signatures are never checked by anyone anywhere | Teachers can't tell one person's writing from another's | Forgery is allowed for children
test: Sophie's dad laughed, then stopped laughing. Why? | He saw it was funny but also a serious lie | He didn't understand the joke | He thought the mistake on the report was his own | He was pleased with the new report
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the signing of a parent's name upon a school notice by a pupil? | It constitutes forgery, a serious deception, ordinarily discovered upon contact with the parent | It constitutes a permissible convenience where the parent is known to agree | It is lawful where the parent is occupied | It is not deception, the content of the notice being accurate and the parent being likely to consent
exam: Identify the principal consequence of the discovery of a single forged notice in respect of a pupil who has submitted several. | The examination of all such notices previously submitted | The disregard of the remaining notices as of no importance | The commendation of the pupil's handwriting | The discontinuation of all notices to that household
exam: It is given that a pupil forged a medical note in order to avoid a swimming lesson which he feared. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The forgery concealed a fear which admitted of a simple remedy | The fear was of no importance and required no attention | The forgery resolved the difficulty permanently and without cost | The pupil was obliged to enter deep water thereafter
exam: Determine the most appropriate conduct of a pupil in possession of a notice requiring a parental signature in respect of uncompleted homework. | To deliver the notice to the parent for signature | To sign the notice on the parent's behalf | To conceal the notice until the deadline has passed | To report the notice as lost on the journey home
